{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f3","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":468,"recorded_at":1780531400000931,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b"},"task_id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md`.\n(3702 line(s) above)\n3703|### 7.A Provider Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding\n3704|\n3705|Provider Protocol 3.0.0 retains the exact v2 framing, request/result envelopes,\n3706|operation registry, idempotency rules, Provider authority, and embedded types,\n3707|except that every occurrence of the v2 <code>TerminalDescriptor</code> is\n3708|replaced by the following closed v3 object:\n3709|\n3710|| Member | Exact type and constraint |\n3711|| --- | --- |\n3712|| <code>terminal_binding_id</code> | digest of the validated Terminal Instance Binding 1.0.0 |\n3713|| <code>terminal_instance_id</code> | AX-assigned UUIDv7; never a PID, socket, path, pipe, URL, handle, token, or native reference |\n3714|| <code>terminal_backend_id</code> | Section 4.B registered backend ID |\n3715|| <code>implementation_version</code> | Semantic Version equal to the admitted Manifest and Probe |\n3716|| <code>protocol_version</code> | Semantic Version in Terminal Backend Protocol major 1 |\n3717|| <code>backend_generation</code> | string[1..256] equal to the host-local binding generation; lengths 0 and 257 are invalid |\n3718|| <code>interactive</code> | boolean |\n3719|| <code>columns</code> / <code>rows</code> | uint16[1..1000] |\n3720|\n3721|No other member is permitted. The descriptor is AX-supplied delegated process-\n3722|hosting context; it conveys no Session ownership, lease, provider identity,\n3723|workspace, checkpoint, replication, or takeover authority to the Provider or\n3724|TerminalBackend. The Provider MUST reject a descriptor whose binding digest,\n3725|backend ID/version, or generation does not match the AX-validated host-local\n3726|binding before launching or observing a provider process. The provider still\n3727|receives the LeaseToken separately and MUST apply its v2 fencing rules.\n3728|\n3729|Provider Protocol 3.0.0 binds Structured Error 1.3.0. A syntactically valid v3\n3730|request receives the v3 envelope with Error 1.3 on failure. A v2/v3 major\n3731|mismatch, an unparseable or oversize first frame, or output that cannot be\n3732|framed follows the Section 15.1 close/termination rule; the caller never trusts\n3733|a different major's error. A dual-stack adapter advertises and validates v2 and\n3734|v3 independently. It MAY translate only <code>ax.tmux</code> and\n3735|<code>ax.conpty</code> to the immutable v2 descriptor under Section 4.E; every\n3736|other backend is <code>incompatible_protocol</code>, not a fallback.\n3737|\n3738|### 7.8 Companion Session Adapter protocol\n3739|\n3740|Cross-environment cloning uses <code>urn:ax:protocol:session-adapter</code>\n3741|1.0.0 in the same trusted <code>ax-provider-&lt;id&gt;</code> executable and with\n3742|the same host-observed executable digest as Provider Protocol 2.0.0. It is not\n3743|a separately discovered plugin. The Session Adapter owns native\n3744|discovery/capture, canonical normalization, isolated projection, and\n3745|independent read-back. Provider 2 and the AX materializer exclusively own\n3746|live-store mutation, rollback custody, publication, and finalization.\n3747|\n3748|The request envelope contains exactly <code>protocol</code>,\n3749|<code>protocol_version</code>, <code>request_id</code>, <code>operation</code>,\n3750|<code>deadline</code>, and <code>body</code>. Success contains the first four\n3751|echoed identity fields, <code>ok=true</code>, and <code>body</code>; failure\n3752|contains them, <code>ok=false</code>, and Structured Error 1.1. Body and error\n3753|are disjoint, never nullable sentinels. The protocol inherits Section 7.2's\n3754|JSONL, 8 MiB, deadline, stderr-redaction, and process rules. Invalid envelopes\n3755|are <code>session_adapter_protocol_error</code>.\n3756|\n3757|The closed operation registry is:\n3758|\n3759|| Operation | Permitted authority/effect |\n3760|| --- | --- |\n3761|| <code>manifest</code>, <code>probe</code> | None |\n3762|| <code>discover</code>, <code>inspect</code> | Read-only declared native roots |\n3763|| <code>snapshot-proof</code> | Read-only proof; runtime control only for an AX-owned source through Provider <code>quiesce</code> |\n3764|| <code>capture-plan</code> | Fresh isolated plan sink |\n3765|| <code>capture</code>, <code>normalize</code> | Fresh isolated raw/canonical Object Sink |\n3766|| <code>projection-plan</code>, <code>project</code> | Fresh isolated plan/target-object sink; never a live store |\n3767|| <code>read-back</code> | Read-only staged or live authority supplied by core |\n3768|| <code>validate</code>, <code>resume-plan</code> | None; resume-plan returns existing-identity argv/cwd/non-secret environment names |\n3769|| <code>doctor</code> | None |\n3770|\n3771|Every adapter implements every name; an unavailable operation returns\n3772|<code>capability_unavailable</code>, and an unknown name returns\n3773|<code>operation_unknown</code>. Bodies are closed and bounded. Every\n3774|state-bearing call includes a host-created context binding operation, provider,\n3775|Environment Tuple, manifest/executable digests, and request digest. Read\n3776|authorities name host-opened handles, purpose, and expiry, never paths. Object\n3777|authorities name purpose, <code>read|fresh_sink</code>, and hard object/byte\n3778|limits. Partial, malformed, over-limit, or escaped results are errors, never\n3779|absence or fallback permission. Core independently validates, hashes, and seals\n3780|every candidate object.\n3781|\n3782|Every operation body is a closed object. In the definitions below,\n3783|<code>T[n..m]</code> is an array with the inclusive item bound and\n3784|<code>sorted unique</code> means bytewise order of each item's JCS encoding.\n3785|Large data is referenced by manifest or Blob Descriptor ID and MUST NOT be\n3786|embedded in the 8 MiB frame. The reusable closed body types are:\n3787|\n3788|- <code>AdapterCallContext</code> contains exactly\n3789|  <code>operation_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>provider_id:provider-id</code>,\n3790|  <code>environment:EnvironmentTuple</code>,\n3791|  <code>session_adapter_manifest_digest:digest</code>,\n3792|  <code>executable_sha256:digest</code>, <code>request_digest:digest</code>,\n3793|  and <code>extensions</code>. The host constructs it from the verified\n3794|  candidate. <code>request_digest</code> is the digest of the JCS request body\n3795|  with only that member omitted. Every success echoes the context byte-for-byte.\n3796|- <code>ReadAuthority</code> contains exactly <code>authority_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n3797|  <code>purpose:source_native|target_staged|target_live</code>,\n3798|  <code>root_handle_names:sorted unique string[1..128][1..128]</code>,\n3799|  <code>expires_at:timestamp</code>, and <code>extensions</code>. Handle names\n3800|  identify host-opened descriptors or handles delivered out of band; they are\n3801|  never paths.\n3802|- <code>ObjectAuthority</code> contains exactly\n3803|  <code>authority_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n3804|  <code>purpose:capture_plan|raw_source|canonical_source|projection_plan|projected_target|read_back_evidence</code>,\n3805|  <code>mode:read|fresh_sink</code>, <code>max_objects:uint53</code>,\n3806|  <code>max_total_bytes:uint53</code>, and <code>extensions</code>.\n3807|  <code>fresh_sink</code> requires an empty host-created sink and both limits\n3808|  greater than zero. <code>read</code> exposes only request-named objects.\n3809|- <code>SourceSelector</code> contains exactly\n3810|  <code>native_session_id:string[1..512]|null</code>,\n3811|  <code>logical_workspace_id:UUIDv7|null</code>, and\n3812|  <code>opaque_source_ref:string[1..512]|null</code>; exactly one is non-null.\n3813|- <code>AdapterFinding</code> contains exactly\n3814|  <code>severity:info|warning|error</code>, <code>code:string[1..128]</code>,\n3815|  <code>message:string[1..4096]</code>,\n3816|  <code>remediation:string[1..4096]|null</code>, and <code>extensions</code>.\n3817|- <code>ResourceLimits</code> contains exactly\n3818|  <code>max_objects:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3819|  <code>max_total_bytes:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3820|  <code>max_single_object_bytes:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3821|  <code>max_events:uint53[0..65536]</code>, and\n3822|  <code>max_target_resources:uint53[0..65536]</code>. Each per-item maximum is\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f5","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":469,"recorded_at":1780531400000933,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b"},"task_id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0b35e-fe47-7b03-b0db-356881355d3b"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f7","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":470,"recorded_at":1780531400000935,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.output","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0"},"task_id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0","event":{"kind":"output","task_id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0","chunk":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md`.\n(9177 line(s) above)\n9178|### 13.1 Direct-session launch\n9179|\n9180|Preconditions:\n9181|\n9182|- configuration and local host identity are valid;\n9183|- the session name is unused across the reachable mesh;\n9184|- the provider plugin is trusted and <code>probe</code> succeeds;\n9185|- requested profile mapping is available;\n9186|- destination workspace is validated or captured as a new managed workspace;\n9187|- the terminal backend is available; and\n9188|- no task-board fields are supplied.\n9189|\n9190|Transitions:\n9191|\n9192|1. Allocate session, caller-stable bootstrap-operation,\n9193|   first-checkpoint-operation, and initial lease IDs. If the workspace has a\n9194|   valid current managed-replica marker, reuse its unique Workspace Group\n9195|   Record after exact topology validation; otherwise allocate new workspace-\n9196|   group/workspace IDs and one new group record. Epoch is 1 and the local host\n9197|   is the holder.\n9198|2. Persist any new Workspace Group Record, then the Session Record referencing\n9199|   that group, the initial Lease Record, and <code>session.created</code> carrying\n9200|   both operation IDs before creating a process. Joining\n9201|   an existing group publishes no group update; derived membership changes by\n9202|   union of the new Session Record.\n9203|3. Create the durable terminal entry <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> with the\n9204|   bootstrap operation ID.\n9205|4. Call provider <code>launch</code> and validate its argv/env-name plan.\n9206|5. Start the provider through the terminal backend with the epoch-1 fencing\n9207|   token and persisted profile. Persist <code>provider.launched</code> with\n9208|   <code>execution_profile</code> equal to the Session Record and\n9209|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>.\n9210|6. Call <code>identify-session</code>; persist the Provider Identity Record and\n9211|   <code>provider.identified</code> event.\n9212|7. When the first safe boundary is observed, create the workspace-group root\n9213|   manifest. If <code>portable_store</code> is enabled, call provider\n9214|   <code>native-store-plan phase=capture</code> and <code>capture</code> with the\n9215|   first-checkpoint operation ID into an\n9216|   isolated Object Sink; otherwise create a zero-entry provider manifest that\n9217|   references the exact Provider Identity Record. Validate both, then publish\n9218|   the direct-path checkpoint with a non-null provider manifest.\n9219|8. Publish immutable objects for replica sync and report <code>running</code>\n9220|   or <code>idle</code>.\n9221|\n9222|When the launch carries a caller-supplied plan (Section 14.1\n9223|<code>--launch-plan</code>), one step precedes step 2 for both document forms.\n9224|<code>ax</code> calls provider <code>launch</code> with the candidate Session\n9225|Record in planning role — provider <code>launch</code> is already a\n9226|plan-returning operation that creates no process (Section 7.5) — takes the\n9227|returned <code>SpawnPlan.argv</code> as the resolved final argv (the plugin's\n9228|base argv followed by the document's <code>argv_suffix</code>; for the\n9229|<code>argv</code> form, the plugin's verbatim translation after its Section\n9230|7.7 profile-flag check), validates it under the Section 5.1 argv limits\n9231|(1..128 elements, each 1–4,096 bytes, at most 65,536 encoded bytes), and only\n9232|then persists the record with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to that\n9233|final argv, so the immutable record answers \"what launched\" without\n9234|re-deriving anything. Every refusal of this step — shape, limit, exclusivity,\n9235|unknown member, extension collision or bound, secret, profile flag, or\n9236|missing capability — fires before any Session Record, lease, terminal entry,\n9237|or process exists. A plugin declaring <code>caller_launch_plan</code> MUST\n9238|answer the planning call deterministically, and step 4's <code>launch</code>\n9239|against the persisted record MUST return an argv equal to the recorded one;\n9240|a mismatch is <code>provider_protocol_error</code> and no process is created.\n9241|\n9242|The record carries the caller document under the <code>ax</code>-generic\n9243|top-level <code>extensions</code> key <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code>, a\n9244|closed object\n9245|<code>{form: \"argv\" | \"argv_suffix\", base_argv_length: uint53, request_digest: digest}</code>.\n9246|The <code>ax</code> label mirrors the <code>urn:ax</code> authority of every\n9247|schema identifier in Section 1.5. The document itself is not stored: the\n9248|Section 1.6 extension bound is 65,536 canonical bytes, and a document may\n9249|carry 65,536 bytes of argv plus 65,536 bytes of stdin. For the same reason the\n9250|value carries no copy of the suffix: <code>launch_plan.argv</code> already\n9251|holds it, and the suffix is defined as\n9252|<code>launch_plan.argv[base_argv_length:]</code> (for the <code>argv</code>\n9253|form <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0 and the suffix is the whole argv).\n9254|That definition is what resume replays (Section 7.5) and what lets a reader\n9255|split the recorded final argv into its two owners; the value is a few dozen\n9256|bytes regardless of the plan's size. <code>request_digest</code> is the\n9257|Section 1.6 SHA-256 digest of the JCS canonical bytes of the caller document\n9258|and lets an operator prove which document produced the record; it is a record,\n9259|not a signature. The caller's own <code>extensions</code> — for a\n9260|Curator-composed document the four <code>works.relux.curator.*</code> keys of\n9261|Section 5.1 are among them; <code>ax</code> is generic and knows no such class\n9262|— together with this key MUST fit the Section 1.6 extensions bound (at most 64\n9263|keys, canonical object at most 65,536 bytes) as they will be persisted; a\n9264|document that would not is refused in the planning step with\n9265|<code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \"extensions\"</code>, so no\n9266|document that passes validation can yield a record <code>ax</code> cannot\n9267|persist.\n9268|\n9269|The caller-plan path has these required conformance cases; Appendix D binds\n9270|them to <code>fixtures/launch_plan_request_conformance.json</code>:\n9271|\n9272|| Fixture | Input | Required result |\n9273|| --- | --- | --- |\n9274|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SUFFIX-POS</code> | <code>argv_suffix</code> document under <code>--profile standard</code> against a <code>caller_launch_plan</code> plugin | Record persisted with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to the plugin's base argv followed by the suffix and <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> <code>{form: \"argv_suffix\", base_argv_length: &lt;base length&gt;, request_digest}</code>; step 4 returns the recorded argv |\n9275|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-ARGV-POS</code> | <code>argv</code> document whose element 0 is the plugin-resolved executable, <code>--profile standard</code> | Plugin appends nothing; <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0; recorded argv equals the document's |\n9276|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-PROFILE-FLAG-NEG</code> | <code>argv</code> or <code>argv_suffix</code> carries the provider's own Section 7.7 <code>yolo</code> flag, long form or documented alias, under <code>--profile standard</code> | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>reason: \"profile_flag\"</code>, <code>details.argv_index</code> the element's index in the final argv; no Session Record or process |\n9277|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SECRET-NEG</code> | An <code>env_literals</code> value or <code>stdin</code> payload the Section 5.1 secret rule or Section 16.2 exclusions classify | <code>secret_policy_violation</code> (exit 16), <code>details.field</code> naming the member; no Session Record |\n9278|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-EXTENSIONS-NEG</code> | Caller <code>extensions</code> that with <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> and the Curator keys exceed the Section 1.6 bound | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \"extensions\"</code>; no Session Record |\n9279|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-CAPABILITY-NEG</code> | Any document against a plugin whose manifest does not declare <code>caller_launch_plan</code> | <code>capability_unavailable</code>, <code>details.capability: \"caller_launch_plan\"</code>, before the plugin is invoked |\n9280|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-DETERMINISM-NEG</code> | Plugin returns at step 4 an argv different from its planning-role answer | <code>provider_protocol_error</code>; no process |\n9281|\n9282|Each negative case is proven by a test that fails when the gate admits the\n9283|input, driven through the real <code>ax start --launch-plan</code> entry point;\n9284|a delete-only mutant is insufficient (Appendix D.1), and the profile-flag case\n9285|MUST also survive a narrowing mutant that drops one documented alias.\n9286|\n9287|The first-checkpoint operation ID keys the capture/export and checkpoint-\n9288|publication transaction even when a backend-only provider emits a zero-entry\n9289|manifest. An identical retry reuses the same immutable objects and event;\n9290|changing either operation ID after <code>session.created</code> is\n9291|<code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\n9292|\n9293|Until step 7 publishes the first Checkpoint, this is the closed\n9294|<em>bootstrap window</em>. The epoch-1 lease is authoritative with\n9295|<code>checkpoint_id = null</code>; the derived session state is\n9296|<code>creating</code> while a retry is eligible and <code>failed</code> after an\n9297|ambiguous/live-process error or explicit abort. No implementation may invent a\n9298|checkpoint ID or call that state <code>stopped</code>.\n9299|\n9300|Failure handling is phase-exact:\n9301|\n9302|| Fixture/boundary | Durable facts | Recovery |\n9303|| --- | --- | --- |\n9304|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-TERMINAL</code> | Session/lease exist; no terminal, process, identity, or checkpoint | <code>ax resume</code> may retry terminal creation under the same IDs after probe; force stop emits bootstrap abort |\n9305|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-TERMINAL</code> | Wrapper exists; no provider process or checkpoint | Resume reuses that wrapper and retries launch once; force stop removes only the inert wrapper after closure proof |\n9306|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-PROCESS</code> | Provider may be live; no identified identity/checkpoint | Resume MUST first reconcile exact PID/PTY and bootstrap operation; if liveness/identity is ambiguous, remain failed and require stop/reconciliation rather than start a second process |\n9307|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-IDENTITY</code> | Identity/event exist; no checkpoint | Resume validates the same identity and continues capture; it MUST NOT call provider new-session launch |\n9308|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-CHECKPOINT</code> | Captured objects may exist but no Checkpoint Record is authoritative | Validate/reuse immutable objects and publish the first checkpoint, or abort; objects are never relabeled as a checkpoint |\n9309|\n9310|A bootstrap retry is allowed only at epoch 1, with a null lease checkpoint,\n9311|no authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code>, and either proven no\n9312|provider process or one exact reconciled process/identity from this launch.\n9313|The retry reuses every already persisted operation/terminal/provider identity\n9314|and is idempotent; it never creates a second raw provider process. A graceful\n9315|stop may first reach a safe boundary and publish the initial checkpoint, then\n9316|execute the ordinary checkpointed stop. A force stop may close the process and\n9317|emit <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> followed by\n9318|<code>session.stopped</code> with null checkpoint,\n9319|<code>resumable=false</code>, and <code>closure_kind=bootstrap_abort</code>; the\n9320|resulting lifecycle state is <code>failed</code>, not <code>stopped</code>.\n9321|Closure with an unknown/live process is unsuccessful and emits neither event.\n9322|\n9323|<code>BOOT-ABORT-CROSS-CONTRACT</code> is the exact successful force-closure\n9324|projection for a failure after process creation and before identity:\n9325|\n9326|| Surface | Exact authoritative values |\n9327|| --- | --- |\n9328|| <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> payload | <code>{operation_id:BOOTSTRAP_ID,failure_phase:after_process,provider_identity_record_id:null,manager_session_ref:null,process_closed:true,store_closed:true,resume_allowed:false}</code> |\n9329|| Following <code>session.stopped</code> payload | <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,closure_kind:bootstrap_abort,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n9330|| RPC <code>session.stop</code> success | <code>{operation_id:STOP_ID,already_applied:false,closure:{process_closed:true,store_closed:true,exit_code:EXIT_OR_NULL,remaining_process_handles:[],final_store_generation:null},final_checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,resulting_state:failed}</code> |\n9331|| CLI <code>stop</code> body | Exact failed <code>SessionSummary</code> with both newest-checkpoint members null, plus <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n9332|\n9333|The event IDs and sequences are ordinary Section 5.2 identities. The RPC and\n9334|CLI operation ID is STOP_ID; the abort event retains BOOTSTRAP_ID so audit can\n9335|join the abandoned launch. <code>BOOT-ABORT-N1</code> changes any one null to a\n9336|digest, any closure boolean to false, resulting state to stopped, or resumable\n9337|to true; the attempted success MUST be rejected. After identity, exactly the\n9338|direct provider-identity or task-board manager-reference member becomes\n9339|non-null as Section 5.2 requires.\n9340|\n9341|The command MUST NOT delete the workspace, native provider bytes, or immutable\n9342|records during recovery. The initial lease remains authoritative until an\n9343|explicit later ownership operation.\n9344|\n9345|### 13.2 Task-board session launch\n9346|\n9347|Preconditions are the direct launch preconditions except that the official\n9348|task-board bridge and the requested task-board capability MUST be available.\n9349|The logical board identity and, when applicable, exact\n9350|<code>board-goal-v2</code> reference/revision MUST be known. The Section 14.1\n9351|board ID/URL, launch mode, goal pair, and binding rules are the only public\n9352|resolution contract; provider ID or current directory MUST NOT silently choose\n9353|them.\n9354|\n9355|Transitions:\n9356|\n9357|1. Allocate distinct caller-stable bootstrap and first-checkpoint operation\n9358|   IDs. Create and durably persist the\n9359|   Workspace Group Record or reuse a marker-validated existing one exactly as\n9360|   Section 13.1, then create and durably persist the\n9361|   <code>kind = task_board</code> Session Record with creation-time\n9362|   <code>manager_session_ref = null</code>, the epoch-1 lease, and\n9363|   <code>session.created</code> carrying both IDs before asking task-board to\n9364|   create a process.\n9365|2. Invoke the Section 9.2 public bridge <code>launch</code> command with the\n9366|   bootstrap operation ID, the exact persisted lease/profile/provider/mode/goal/launch\n9367|   plan, logical workspace mapping, and no provider-private mutation.\n9368|3. Task-board returns a public manager session reference, exact provider\n9369|   version, ax binding, and capability result. Validate every repeated input,\n9370|   then persist\n9371|   <code>task_board.launched</code> with the Session Record profile and\n9372|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>; this event, not an edit to the Session\n9373|   Record, establishes the current manager reference.\n9374|4. At a manager-proven safe boundary, invoke official\n9375|   <code>session export --mode snapshot</code> with the persisted first-checkpoint\n9376|   operation ID from <code>session.created</code>. Require the exact Section 9.2\n9377|   BridgeSafeBoundary with <code>safe = true</code>; input is released only\n(more lines below)","final_result":true}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6f9","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":471,"recorded_at":1780531400000937,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"task.lifecycle.completed","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"task_lifecycle","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"task_stream":{"kind":"task","id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0"},"task_id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0","event":{"kind":"completed","task_id":"01a0b35e-fe74-7b81-ba61-9212426191d0"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6fb","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":472,"recorded_at":1780531400000939,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"call_id":"call_01a0b35ef86f773ea1c28e5a41c70651","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md`.\n(3702 line(s) above)\n3703|### 7.A Provider Protocol 3.0.0 Terminal Instance binding\n3704|\n3705|Provider Protocol 3.0.0 retains the exact v2 framing, request/result envelopes,\n3706|operation registry, idempotency rules, Provider authority, and embedded types,\n3707|except that every occurrence of the v2 <code>TerminalDescriptor</code> is\n3708|replaced by the following closed v3 object:\n3709|\n3710|| Member | Exact type and constraint |\n3711|| --- | --- |\n3712|| <code>terminal_binding_id</code> | digest of the validated Terminal Instance Binding 1.0.0 |\n3713|| <code>terminal_instance_id</code> | AX-assigned UUIDv7; never a PID, socket, path, pipe, URL, handle, token, or native reference |\n3714|| <code>terminal_backend_id</code> | Section 4.B registered backend ID |\n3715|| <code>implementation_version</code> | Semantic Version equal to the admitted Manifest and Probe |\n3716|| <code>protocol_version</code> | Semantic Version in Terminal Backend Protocol major 1 |\n3717|| <code>backend_generation</code> | string[1..256] equal to the host-local binding generation; lengths 0 and 257 are invalid |\n3718|| <code>interactive</code> | boolean |\n3719|| <code>columns</code> / <code>rows</code> | uint16[1..1000] |\n3720|\n3721|No other member is permitted. The descriptor is AX-supplied delegated process-\n3722|hosting context; it conveys no Session ownership, lease, provider identity,\n3723|workspace, checkpoint, replication, or takeover authority to the Provider or\n3724|TerminalBackend. The Provider MUST reject a descriptor whose binding digest,\n3725|backend ID/version, or generation does not match the AX-validated host-local\n3726|binding before launching or observing a provider process. The provider still\n3727|receives the LeaseToken separately and MUST apply its v2 fencing rules.\n3728|\n3729|Provider Protocol 3.0.0 binds Structured Error 1.3.0. A syntactically valid v3\n3730|request receives the v3 envelope with Error 1.3 on failure. A v2/v3 major\n3731|mismatch, an unparseable or oversize first frame, or output that cannot be\n3732|framed follows the Section 15.1 close/termination rule; the caller never trusts\n3733|a different major's error. A dual-stack adapter advertises and validates v2 and\n3734|v3 independently. It MAY translate only <code>ax.tmux</code> and\n3735|<code>ax.conpty</code> to the immutable v2 descriptor under Section 4.E; every\n3736|other backend is <code>incompatible_protocol</code>, not a fallback.\n3737|\n3738|### 7.8 Companion Session Adapter protocol\n3739|\n3740|Cross-environment cloning uses <code>urn:ax:protocol:session-adapter</code>\n3741|1.0.0 in the same trusted <code>ax-provider-&lt;id&gt;</code> executable and with\n3742|the same host-observed executable digest as Provider Protocol 2.0.0. It is not\n3743|a separately discovered plugin. The Session Adapter owns native\n3744|discovery/capture, canonical normalization, isolated projection, and\n3745|independent read-back. Provider 2 and the AX materializer exclusively own\n3746|live-store mutation, rollback custody, publication, and finalization.\n3747|\n3748|The request envelope contains exactly <code>protocol</code>,\n3749|<code>protocol_version</code>, <code>request_id</code>, <code>operation</code>,\n3750|<code>deadline</code>, and <code>body</code>. Success contains the first four\n3751|echoed identity fields, <code>ok=true</code>, and <code>body</code>; failure\n3752|contains them, <code>ok=false</code>, and Structured Error 1.1. Body and error\n3753|are disjoint, never nullable sentinels. The protocol inherits Section 7.2's\n3754|JSONL, 8 MiB, deadline, stderr-redaction, and process rules. Invalid envelopes\n3755|are <code>session_adapter_protocol_error</code>.\n3756|\n3757|The closed operation registry is:\n3758|\n3759|| Operation | Permitted authority/effect |\n3760|| --- | --- |\n3761|| <code>manifest</code>, <code>probe</code> | None |\n3762|| <code>discover</code>, <code>inspect</code> | Read-only declared native roots |\n3763|| <code>snapshot-proof</code> | Read-only proof; runtime control only for an AX-owned source through Provider <code>quiesce</code> |\n3764|| <code>capture-plan</code> | Fresh isolated plan sink |\n3765|| <code>capture</code>, <code>normalize</code> | Fresh isolated raw/canonical Object Sink |\n3766|| <code>projection-plan</code>, <code>project</code> | Fresh isolated plan/target-object sink; never a live store |\n3767|| <code>read-back</code> | Read-only staged or live authority supplied by core |\n3768|| <code>validate</code>, <code>resume-plan</code> | None; resume-plan returns existing-identity argv/cwd/non-secret environment names |\n3769|| <code>doctor</code> | None |\n3770|\n3771|Every adapter implements every name; an unavailable operation returns\n3772|<code>capability_unavailable</code>, and an unknown name returns\n3773|<code>operation_unknown</code>. Bodies are closed and bounded. Every\n3774|state-bearing call includes a host-created context binding operation, provider,\n3775|Environment Tuple, manifest/executable digests, and request digest. Read\n3776|authorities name host-opened handles, purpose, and expiry, never paths. Object\n3777|authorities name purpose, <code>read|fresh_sink</code>, and hard object/byte\n3778|limits. Partial, malformed, over-limit, or escaped results are errors, never\n3779|absence or fallback permission. Core independently validates, hashes, and seals\n3780|every candidate object.\n3781|\n3782|Every operation body is a closed object. In the definitions below,\n3783|<code>T[n..m]</code> is an array with the inclusive item bound and\n3784|<code>sorted unique</code> means bytewise order of each item's JCS encoding.\n3785|Large data is referenced by manifest or Blob Descriptor ID and MUST NOT be\n3786|embedded in the 8 MiB frame. The reusable closed body types are:\n3787|\n3788|- <code>AdapterCallContext</code> contains exactly\n3789|  <code>operation_id:UUIDv7</code>, <code>provider_id:provider-id</code>,\n3790|  <code>environment:EnvironmentTuple</code>,\n3791|  <code>session_adapter_manifest_digest:digest</code>,\n3792|  <code>executable_sha256:digest</code>, <code>request_digest:digest</code>,\n3793|  and <code>extensions</code>. The host constructs it from the verified\n3794|  candidate. <code>request_digest</code> is the digest of the JCS request body\n3795|  with only that member omitted. Every success echoes the context byte-for-byte.\n3796|- <code>ReadAuthority</code> contains exactly <code>authority_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n3797|  <code>purpose:source_native|target_staged|target_live</code>,\n3798|  <code>root_handle_names:sorted unique string[1..128][1..128]</code>,\n3799|  <code>expires_at:timestamp</code>, and <code>extensions</code>. Handle names\n3800|  identify host-opened descriptors or handles delivered out of band; they are\n3801|  never paths.\n3802|- <code>ObjectAuthority</code> contains exactly\n3803|  <code>authority_id:UUIDv7</code>,\n3804|  <code>purpose:capture_plan|raw_source|canonical_source|projection_plan|projected_target|read_back_evidence</code>,\n3805|  <code>mode:read|fresh_sink</code>, <code>max_objects:uint53</code>,\n3806|  <code>max_total_bytes:uint53</code>, and <code>extensions</code>.\n3807|  <code>fresh_sink</code> requires an empty host-created sink and both limits\n3808|  greater than zero. <code>read</code> exposes only request-named objects.\n3809|- <code>SourceSelector</code> contains exactly\n3810|  <code>native_session_id:string[1..512]|null</code>,\n3811|  <code>logical_workspace_id:UUIDv7|null</code>, and\n3812|  <code>opaque_source_ref:string[1..512]|null</code>; exactly one is non-null.\n3813|- <code>AdapterFinding</code> contains exactly\n3814|  <code>severity:info|warning|error</code>, <code>code:string[1..128]</code>,\n3815|  <code>message:string[1..4096]</code>,\n3816|  <code>remediation:string[1..4096]|null</code>, and <code>extensions</code>.\n3817|- <code>ResourceLimits</code> contains exactly\n3818|  <code>max_objects:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3819|  <code>max_total_bytes:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3820|  <code>max_single_object_bytes:uint53&gt;0</code>,\n3821|  <code>max_events:uint53[0..65536]</code>, and\n3822|  <code>max_target_resources:uint53[0..65536]</code>. Each per-item maximum is\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c6fd","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0b35d-fadf-7f82-925c-5ba6aefcf0a7"},"sequence":473,"recorded_at":1780531400000941,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"38ee7473-f77a-4937-9e67-866e0a213216"},"call_id":"call_01a0b35efa6a710ab2314beffcd72d17","text":"Read text file `internal/specdoc/SPEC.v0.7.0.md`.\n(9177 line(s) above)\n9178|### 13.1 Direct-session launch\n9179|\n9180|Preconditions:\n9181|\n9182|- configuration and local host identity are valid;\n9183|- the session name is unused across the reachable mesh;\n9184|- the provider plugin is trusted and <code>probe</code> succeeds;\n9185|- requested profile mapping is available;\n9186|- destination workspace is validated or captured as a new managed workspace;\n9187|- the terminal backend is available; and\n9188|- no task-board fields are supplied.\n9189|\n9190|Transitions:\n9191|\n9192|1. Allocate session, caller-stable bootstrap-operation,\n9193|   first-checkpoint-operation, and initial lease IDs. If the workspace has a\n9194|   valid current managed-replica marker, reuse its unique Workspace Group\n9195|   Record after exact topology validation; otherwise allocate new workspace-\n9196|   group/workspace IDs and one new group record. Epoch is 1 and the local host\n9197|   is the holder.\n9198|2. Persist any new Workspace Group Record, then the Session Record referencing\n9199|   that group, the initial Lease Record, and <code>session.created</code> carrying\n9200|   both operation IDs before creating a process. Joining\n9201|   an existing group publishes no group update; derived membership changes by\n9202|   union of the new Session Record.\n9203|3. Create the durable terminal entry <code>ax pane SESSION_ID</code> with the\n9204|   bootstrap operation ID.\n9205|4. Call provider <code>launch</code> and validate its argv/env-name plan.\n9206|5. Start the provider through the terminal backend with the epoch-1 fencing\n9207|   token and persisted profile. Persist <code>provider.launched</code> with\n9208|   <code>execution_profile</code> equal to the Session Record and\n9209|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>.\n9210|6. Call <code>identify-session</code>; persist the Provider Identity Record and\n9211|   <code>provider.identified</code> event.\n9212|7. When the first safe boundary is observed, create the workspace-group root\n9213|   manifest. If <code>portable_store</code> is enabled, call provider\n9214|   <code>native-store-plan phase=capture</code> and <code>capture</code> with the\n9215|   first-checkpoint operation ID into an\n9216|   isolated Object Sink; otherwise create a zero-entry provider manifest that\n9217|   references the exact Provider Identity Record. Validate both, then publish\n9218|   the direct-path checkpoint with a non-null provider manifest.\n9219|8. Publish immutable objects for replica sync and report <code>running</code>\n9220|   or <code>idle</code>.\n9221|\n9222|When the launch carries a caller-supplied plan (Section 14.1\n9223|<code>--launch-plan</code>), one step precedes step 2 for both document forms.\n9224|<code>ax</code> calls provider <code>launch</code> with the candidate Session\n9225|Record in planning role — provider <code>launch</code> is already a\n9226|plan-returning operation that creates no process (Section 7.5) — takes the\n9227|returned <code>SpawnPlan.argv</code> as the resolved final argv (the plugin's\n9228|base argv followed by the document's <code>argv_suffix</code>; for the\n9229|<code>argv</code> form, the plugin's verbatim translation after its Section\n9230|7.7 profile-flag check), validates it under the Section 5.1 argv limits\n9231|(1..128 elements, each 1–4,096 bytes, at most 65,536 encoded bytes), and only\n9232|then persists the record with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to that\n9233|final argv, so the immutable record answers \"what launched\" without\n9234|re-deriving anything. Every refusal of this step — shape, limit, exclusivity,\n9235|unknown member, extension collision or bound, secret, profile flag, or\n9236|missing capability — fires before any Session Record, lease, terminal entry,\n9237|or process exists. A plugin declaring <code>caller_launch_plan</code> MUST\n9238|answer the planning call deterministically, and step 4's <code>launch</code>\n9239|against the persisted record MUST return an argv equal to the recorded one;\n9240|a mismatch is <code>provider_protocol_error</code> and no process is created.\n9241|\n9242|The record carries the caller document under the <code>ax</code>-generic\n9243|top-level <code>extensions</code> key <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code>, a\n9244|closed object\n9245|<code>{form: \"argv\" | \"argv_suffix\", base_argv_length: uint53, request_digest: digest}</code>.\n9246|The <code>ax</code> label mirrors the <code>urn:ax</code> authority of every\n9247|schema identifier in Section 1.5. The document itself is not stored: the\n9248|Section 1.6 extension bound is 65,536 canonical bytes, and a document may\n9249|carry 65,536 bytes of argv plus 65,536 bytes of stdin. For the same reason the\n9250|value carries no copy of the suffix: <code>launch_plan.argv</code> already\n9251|holds it, and the suffix is defined as\n9252|<code>launch_plan.argv[base_argv_length:]</code> (for the <code>argv</code>\n9253|form <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0 and the suffix is the whole argv).\n9254|That definition is what resume replays (Section 7.5) and what lets a reader\n9255|split the recorded final argv into its two owners; the value is a few dozen\n9256|bytes regardless of the plan's size. <code>request_digest</code> is the\n9257|Section 1.6 SHA-256 digest of the JCS canonical bytes of the caller document\n9258|and lets an operator prove which document produced the record; it is a record,\n9259|not a signature. The caller's own <code>extensions</code> — for a\n9260|Curator-composed document the four <code>works.relux.curator.*</code> keys of\n9261|Section 5.1 are among them; <code>ax</code> is generic and knows no such class\n9262|— together with this key MUST fit the Section 1.6 extensions bound (at most 64\n9263|keys, canonical object at most 65,536 bytes) as they will be persisted; a\n9264|document that would not is refused in the planning step with\n9265|<code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \"extensions\"</code>, so no\n9266|document that passes validation can yield a record <code>ax</code> cannot\n9267|persist.\n9268|\n9269|The caller-plan path has these required conformance cases; Appendix D binds\n9270|them to <code>fixtures/launch_plan_request_conformance.json</code>:\n9271|\n9272|| Fixture | Input | Required result |\n9273|| --- | --- | --- |\n9274|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SUFFIX-POS</code> | <code>argv_suffix</code> document under <code>--profile standard</code> against a <code>caller_launch_plan</code> plugin | Record persisted with <code>launch_plan.argv</code> equal to the plugin's base argv followed by the suffix and <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> <code>{form: \"argv_suffix\", base_argv_length: &lt;base length&gt;, request_digest}</code>; step 4 returns the recorded argv |\n9275|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-ARGV-POS</code> | <code>argv</code> document whose element 0 is the plugin-resolved executable, <code>--profile standard</code> | Plugin appends nothing; <code>base_argv_length</code> is 0; recorded argv equals the document's |\n9276|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-PROFILE-FLAG-NEG</code> | <code>argv</code> or <code>argv_suffix</code> carries the provider's own Section 7.7 <code>yolo</code> flag, long form or documented alias, under <code>--profile standard</code> | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>reason: \"profile_flag\"</code>, <code>details.argv_index</code> the element's index in the final argv; no Session Record or process |\n9277|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-SECRET-NEG</code> | An <code>env_literals</code> value or <code>stdin</code> payload the Section 5.1 secret rule or Section 16.2 exclusions classify | <code>secret_policy_violation</code> (exit 16), <code>details.field</code> naming the member; no Session Record |\n9278|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-EXTENSIONS-NEG</code> | Caller <code>extensions</code> that with <code>ax.launch-plan-request</code> and the Curator keys exceed the Section 1.6 bound | <code>launch_plan_invalid</code>, <code>field: \"extensions\"</code>; no Session Record |\n9279|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-CAPABILITY-NEG</code> | Any document against a plugin whose manifest does not declare <code>caller_launch_plan</code> | <code>capability_unavailable</code>, <code>details.capability: \"caller_launch_plan\"</code>, before the plugin is invoked |\n9280|| <code>LAUNCH-PLAN-DETERMINISM-NEG</code> | Plugin returns at step 4 an argv different from its planning-role answer | <code>provider_protocol_error</code>; no process |\n9281|\n9282|Each negative case is proven by a test that fails when the gate admits the\n9283|input, driven through the real <code>ax start --launch-plan</code> entry point;\n9284|a delete-only mutant is insufficient (Appendix D.1), and the profile-flag case\n9285|MUST also survive a narrowing mutant that drops one documented alias.\n9286|\n9287|The first-checkpoint operation ID keys the capture/export and checkpoint-\n9288|publication transaction even when a backend-only provider emits a zero-entry\n9289|manifest. An identical retry reuses the same immutable objects and event;\n9290|changing either operation ID after <code>session.created</code> is\n9291|<code>idempotency_mismatch</code>.\n9292|\n9293|Until step 7 publishes the first Checkpoint, this is the closed\n9294|<em>bootstrap window</em>. The epoch-1 lease is authoritative with\n9295|<code>checkpoint_id = null</code>; the derived session state is\n9296|<code>creating</code> while a retry is eligible and <code>failed</code> after an\n9297|ambiguous/live-process error or explicit abort. No implementation may invent a\n9298|checkpoint ID or call that state <code>stopped</code>.\n9299|\n9300|Failure handling is phase-exact:\n9301|\n9302|| Fixture/boundary | Durable facts | Recovery |\n9303|| --- | --- | --- |\n9304|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-TERMINAL</code> | Session/lease exist; no terminal, process, identity, or checkpoint | <code>ax resume</code> may retry terminal creation under the same IDs after probe; force stop emits bootstrap abort |\n9305|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-TERMINAL</code> | Wrapper exists; no provider process or checkpoint | Resume reuses that wrapper and retries launch once; force stop removes only the inert wrapper after closure proof |\n9306|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-PROCESS</code> | Provider may be live; no identified identity/checkpoint | Resume MUST first reconcile exact PID/PTY and bootstrap operation; if liveness/identity is ambiguous, remain failed and require stop/reconciliation rather than start a second process |\n9307|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-AFTER-IDENTITY</code> | Identity/event exist; no checkpoint | Resume validates the same identity and continues capture; it MUST NOT call provider new-session launch |\n9308|| <code>BOOT-DIRECT-BEFORE-CHECKPOINT</code> | Captured objects may exist but no Checkpoint Record is authoritative | Validate/reuse immutable objects and publish the first checkpoint, or abort; objects are never relabeled as a checkpoint |\n9309|\n9310|A bootstrap retry is allowed only at epoch 1, with a null lease checkpoint,\n9311|no authoritative <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code>, and either proven no\n9312|provider process or one exact reconciled process/identity from this launch.\n9313|The retry reuses every already persisted operation/terminal/provider identity\n9314|and is idempotent; it never creates a second raw provider process. A graceful\n9315|stop may first reach a safe boundary and publish the initial checkpoint, then\n9316|execute the ordinary checkpointed stop. A force stop may close the process and\n9317|emit <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> followed by\n9318|<code>session.stopped</code> with null checkpoint,\n9319|<code>resumable=false</code>, and <code>closure_kind=bootstrap_abort</code>; the\n9320|resulting lifecycle state is <code>failed</code>, not <code>stopped</code>.\n9321|Closure with an unknown/live process is unsuccessful and emits neither event.\n9322|\n9323|<code>BOOT-ABORT-CROSS-CONTRACT</code> is the exact successful force-closure\n9324|projection for a failure after process creation and before identity:\n9325|\n9326|| Surface | Exact authoritative values |\n9327|| --- | --- |\n9328|| <code>session.bootstrap_aborted</code> payload | <code>{operation_id:BOOTSTRAP_ID,failure_phase:after_process,provider_identity_record_id:null,manager_session_ref:null,process_closed:true,store_closed:true,resume_allowed:false}</code> |\n9329|| Following <code>session.stopped</code> payload | <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,closure_kind:bootstrap_abort,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n9330|| RPC <code>session.stop</code> success | <code>{operation_id:STOP_ID,already_applied:false,closure:{process_closed:true,store_closed:true,exit_code:EXIT_OR_NULL,remaining_process_handles:[],final_store_generation:null},final_checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,resulting_state:failed}</code> |\n9331|| CLI <code>stop</code> body | Exact failed <code>SessionSummary</code> with both newest-checkpoint members null, plus <code>{graceful:false,checkpoint_id:null,resumable:false,bootstrap_aborted:true,process_closed:true,store_closed:true}</code> |\n9332|\n9333|The event IDs and sequences are ordinary Section 5.2 identities. The RPC and\n9334|CLI operation ID is STOP_ID; the abort event retains BOOTSTRAP_ID so audit can\n9335|join the abandoned launch. <code>BOOT-ABORT-N1</code> changes any one null to a\n9336|digest, any closure boolean to false, resulting state to stopped, or resumable\n9337|to true; the attempted success MUST be rejected. After identity, exactly the\n9338|direct provider-identity or task-board manager-reference member becomes\n9339|non-null as Section 5.2 requires.\n9340|\n9341|The command MUST NOT delete the workspace, native provider bytes, or immutable\n9342|records during recovery. The initial lease remains authoritative until an\n9343|explicit later ownership operation.\n9344|\n9345|### 13.2 Task-board session launch\n9346|\n9347|Preconditions are the direct launch preconditions except that the official\n9348|task-board bridge and the requested task-board capability MUST be available.\n9349|The logical board identity and, when applicable, exact\n9350|<code>board-goal-v2</code> reference/revision MUST be known. The Section 14.1\n9351|board ID/URL, launch mode, goal pair, and binding rules are the only public\n9352|resolution contract; provider ID or current directory MUST NOT silently choose\n9353|them.\n9354|\n9355|Transitions:\n9356|\n9357|1. Allocate distinct caller-stable bootstrap and first-checkpoint operation\n9358|   IDs. Create and durably persist the\n9359|   Workspace Group Record or reuse a marker-validated existing one exactly as\n9360|   Section 13.1, then create and durably persist the\n9361|   <code>kind = task_board</code> Session Record with creation-time\n9362|   <code>manager_session_ref = null</code>, the epoch-1 lease, and\n9363|   <code>session.created</code> carrying both IDs before asking task-board to\n9364|   create a process.\n9365|2. Invoke the Section 9.2 public bridge <code>launch</code> command with the\n9366|   bootstrap operation ID, the exact persisted lease/profile/provider/mode/goal/launch\n9367|   plan, logical workspace mapping, and no provider-private mutation.\n9368|3. Task-board returns a public manager session reference, exact provider\n9369|   version, ax binding, and capability result. Validate every repeated input,\n9370|   then persist\n9371|   <code>task_board.launched</code> with the Session Record profile and\n9372|   <code>profile_source_event_id = null</code>; this event, not an edit to the Session\n9373|   Record, establishes the current manager reference.\n9374|4. At a manager-proven safe boundary, invoke official\n9375|   <code>session export --mode snapshot</code> with the persisted first-checkpoint\n9376|   operation ID from <code>session.created</code>. Require the exact Section 9.2\n9377|   BridgeSafeBoundary with <code>safe = true</code>; input is released only\n(more lines below)","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
